that well what  I asked for  I was interested in intelligence work  because I it appealed to me because this  sort of putting puzzles together was  something that I really enjoyed doing  and I thought I would enjoy the work and  that was the only place that there was  an intelligence job available when when  I was assigned to there to the flagship  and  Persian Gulf I didn't know where the  Persian Gulf was I had to get out of map  and and find out where it was and I had  a great geography lesson in a year of  sailing around the Indian Ocean and  Persian Gulf places that were  wonderfully exotic and colorful and I  know in a sense for a small-town boy  from from Kansas it was sort of join the  Navy and see the world and I've seen an  awful part of the world where did you go  after that first year from the Persian  Gulf  I went curiously enough for period in  Paris with some language training and  then went down to Morocco and was  working in the there was a naval  installation a Navy base in Morocco that  put out a monthly magazine on political  events in the Middle East and so I spent  it was like up like a year of being a  reporter on a magazine and I learned a  lot about the Middle East and of course  that year read a lot and wrote a lot and  I enjoyed writing it was a great joy to  be able to turn out articles every two  weeks or something like that and what  are you coming on to be a full  lieutenant pretty soon at that point I  was I guess I was I made lieutenant I  guess while I was while I was in Morocco  where from there well then I went up had  a wonderful I got married while I was in  Morocco and my wife had been at Kansas  University with me and we went back got  married and then traveled through France  for a wonderful month and then came back  to the States and I had a series of  assignments in in Washington I went to  the Naval Intelligence School in here in  Washington and then later went out to  Fort Meade Maryland and was watching  Soviet missiles and naval activities  from that vantage point and then went  off to Cairo is the assistant naval  attache  I was in Cairo from 65 to 67 was there  at the beginning of the arab-israeli war  the six-day war I watched it from the  other end the receiving end and stand on  the roof of the embassy in Cairo and  watch the Israeli planes coming in to  hit the desert sites yeah literally  within sight flying around on the edge  the outskirts of the city  shrapnel falling on your head from  overseer from above as they were firing  off flak guns trying to knock down these  airplanes and made uh I was in one of  the people that was involved in setting  up the evacuation of Americans from from  Egypt drove down to Cairo and tried to  organize an evacuation and very exciting  period of time I loved Egypt I still do  and have been back a few times since  that time my daughter was born in Egypt  how many kids to have a son and a  daughter after Cairo what in the Navy  Cairo I came back W we were evacuated  from there spent a short time in Greece  and ended up in Italy with the sixth  fleet and again was stationed in Naples  loved Italy and my daughter loved it so  much that she's gone back and she now  lives in Italy become an Italian  actually she's her Italian is just  perfect we left there came back I went  to the Naval War College and then after  that I got a master's degree in  international relations while I was at  the War College and then went to  Colombia that the Navy has this  wonderful program that a few people a  year are funded to get PhDs and I was  lucky enough to be one of those and went  to Colombia for three years did all the  coursework in the dissertation finished  my PhD came down to Washington I wrote  my my dissertation on the notion great  power politics in the Indian Ocean and  that got me a job in the office of the  Secretary of Defense working on the  Persian Gulf and Indian Ocean issues  what year is this now that was in 1973  to 76 and the secretary defense is well  now Larry James Schlesinger was  James Schlesinger camión Hall I was  there that would basically Schlessinger  time  that's the guy I worked for most there  were there were other people before but  it was really Schlesinger that was the I  wrote some stuff for him and was  involved with with him but he had  commander at this point in the Navy yep  I was a commander at that time yeah  that's right and then went from there  because of my work in that in the  Secretary of Defense's circle system of  the security affairs I got to know a  number of people in the White House in  the National Security Council and when  President Ford was facing election as  often happens people jump ship they get  out while they're rolodex is still hot  and go to work for a consulting firm or  something and then after the election  people flood back in again and they're  always a lot of holes in over there in  the White House staff during that last  year or so six months to a year and so I  was invited to come over to the NSC  staff I thought that's a pretty bad  offer I'll go over there work for six  months it kicked out when the new people  come in whichever way the election goes  and then I'll be out of a job and I'll  have to go scrambling but I did it  anyway I thought I thought it would be a  great place to watch an election from  and it turned out to be a great place to  watch the election from the Carter  people came in after the election I  thought I would be sent back over to the  Department of Defense and they said stay  on it captain yet I was not but I was  then in before the end of the Carter  Administration I was promoted to captain  and that's the rank you left the Navy  what's the right Kartha maybe you're on  the NSC staff how many people are on the  staff at that point about so I recalled  around 50 professionals it's pretty  small you write in the conclusion  although I was a Democrat I had served  nearly half of my career under  Republican presidents why did you feel  the need to put that in the book because  I know that I'm going to be accused of  political partisanship people are going  to say this is a political hatchet job  you're just going after George Bush you  want to embarrass him it's a political  season etc and there's no way I can  avoid that  I mean I know that that charge is going  to go with it  the way I actually got into this book  though was not a carefully plotted event  but it does come at a political time it  is going to be seen as a political  document and so I at least want to make  my position clear I mean that this is  where I am and I have always been a  Democrat and though I've never worked in  political campaigns I've never been a an  activist and I think most people these  until this book came out probably  regarded me is generally nonpartisan I'd  never really participated in partisan  political activities this book is likely  to be used in a partisan way it's not  written in a partisan way but it's  likely to be used in a partisan way and  there's really nothing I can do about  that did you find many Democrats in the  Navy and in the officer corps we didn't  talk politics  seriously no we really didn't talk  politics very much and I honestly  couldn't answer your question I mean I I  I know that some people were Democrats  and some people Republicans but to say  did you meet a lot of them I simply  didn't identify people that I work with  as Republicans or Democrats you say in  the book and I know that I can find the  there's a line here and you know what  I'm talking about where you say that you  oh he said I would not be human if I did  not confess that I have at one time or  another awakened in the middle of the  night with the thought what if all these  people are lying to me is it possible  that all these accounts are themselves a  conspiracy of lies why did you put that  because it's true you can't work on a  story this big this complicated in this  dangerous dangerous to your 